1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,273
Installing software on Linux takes one command.

2
00:00:03,293 --> 00:00:07,274
The real skill is knowing what you installed, where it came from,

3
00:00:07,294 --> 00:00:08,444
and how to undo it.

4
00:00:08,914 --> 00:00:13,054
In this video: install and remove RPM software packages.

5
00:00:17,574 --> 00:00:20,157
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:20,627 --> 00:00:23,700
Install and remove RPM software packages.

7
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:28,747
We'll look before we install, install from a repository, install from a file,

8
00:00:28,767 --> 00:00:31,851
see what a package put on the system, and remove it.

9
00:00:39,371 --> 00:00:45,175
Two tools work with packages. rpm talks to the database of installed packages.

10
00:00:45,195 --> 00:00:46,444
dnf goes further:

11
00:00:46,464 --> 00:00:50,178
it finds packages in repositories, downloads them,

12
00:00:50,198 --> 00:00:52,940
and installs whatever they depend on.

13
00:00:55,410 --> 00:00:58,047
So we use dnf to install and remove,

14
00:00:58,067 --> 00:01:01,662
and rpm to ask questions about what is installed.

15
00:01:08,182 --> 00:01:13,868
Before installing, check. rpm -q tree asks: is the package tree installed?

16
00:01:13,888 --> 00:01:15,565
The answer: it is not.

17
00:01:21,085 --> 00:01:24,779
dnf info shows the details of an available package:

18
00:01:24,799 --> 00:01:29,658
its version, its size, the repository it comes from, and a summary.

19
00:01:29,678 --> 00:01:32,862
The repository metadata lines are not shown.

20
00:01:40,382 --> 00:01:44,408
dnf search looks for a word in package names and summaries.

21
00:01:44,428 --> 00:01:49,962
The exact match, tree, comes first. The list is long, so only the start is shown.

22
00:01:56,482 --> 00:02:01,582
sudo dnf install adds the package. -y answers yes to the question.

23
00:02:01,602 --> 00:02:04,142
The download output is shortened.

24
00:02:09,612 --> 00:02:14,755
rpm -q now shows the exact version installed, and the command works.

25
00:02:21,275 --> 00:02:24,840
rpm -ql lists every file a package installed:

26
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:28,743
the program, its documentation, and its man page.

27
00:02:32,213 --> 00:02:38,102
rpm -qf goes the other way. Give it a file, and it names the package that owns it.

28
00:02:43,622 --> 00:02:46,541
You can also install from a package file.

29
00:02:46,561 --> 00:02:49,767
dnf download saves one without installing it.

30
00:02:49,787 --> 00:02:53,351
We download zip, and ls confirms the file is here.

31
00:02:59,871 --> 00:03:05,147
dnf install with ./ and the file name installs from the file. Look:

32
00:03:05,167 --> 00:03:09,179
it also installed unzip, because zip depends on it.

33
00:03:09,199 --> 00:03:12,736
dnf found unzip in the repository on its own.

34
00:03:18,206 --> 00:03:20,426
rpm -q zip confirms it.

35
00:03:25,946 --> 00:03:31,878
To remove, use dnf remove. Same pattern: sudo, -y, and the package name.

36
00:03:37,348 --> 00:03:42,661
Now rpm -q says tree is not installed, and the shell cannot find the command.

37
00:03:48,181 --> 00:03:50,284
dnf history lists every change:

38
00:03:50,304 --> 00:03:54,735
its number, the command, the date, and how many packages changed.

39
00:03:54,755 --> 00:03:58,913
Our install, install from file, and remove are the top three.

40
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:11,935
One more tool. dnf provides finds which package supplies a file. Here, unzip.

41
00:04:11,955 --> 00:04:16,525
The repository called at System means it is installed right now.

42
00:04:22,995 --> 00:04:26,452
This is how you find what to install when a command is missing.

43
00:04:30,972 --> 00:04:34,408
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

44
00:04:35,928 --> 00:04:39,483
Installed packages live on disk, so they survive a reboot.

45
00:04:39,503 --> 00:04:42,009
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

46
00:04:45,479 --> 00:04:49,086
After a fresh boot, zip is still installed, and tree is still gone.

47
00:04:56,606 --> 00:04:59,356
Your turn. Create a folder lab13.

48
00:04:59,376 --> 00:05:04,978
Use dnf provides to find the package that supplies /usr/bin/telnet,

49
00:05:04,998 --> 00:05:11,105
and write its name on line one of lab13/answer.txt. Install that package.

50
00:05:11,125 --> 00:05:14,378
Then install tree, and remove it again.

51
00:05:17,848 --> 00:05:20,665
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

52
00:05:26,185 --> 00:05:29,578
Next up: Configure access to Flatpak repositories.

53
00:05:30,048 --> 00:05:32,951
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
